---
title: The Profitable Thing / The Other Thing
authors: [Rhoda Ayele]
broadcast: 5
order: 1
---

These precedents look at organizations where a highly desirable commercial activity supports a different kind of cultural or social production. Rather than separating commerce from culture, they place different economic and cultural values within the same ecosystem.

In Los Angeles, Community Goods developed an ultra trendy cafe culture around a small physical space. The café became known for its long lines, recognizable cup branding, and celebrity following, despite reportedly having no traditional marketing budget. The designer and founder Pedro Cavaliere brought an existing network from his work in music and entertainment, including relationships developed while working as a personal assistant to A-Trak and as a tour DJ for Rihanna. This social network became part of the cafes visibility and helped establish Community Goods as a cultural destination in Los Angeles.

Next to the cafe is ARCHITECTURE, an independent archival bookstore focused on architecture, art, and design. The bookstore carries rare and out-of-print publications and hosts exhibitions, book launches, and gatherings. The two spaces have very different levels of visibility and audience - Community Goods is designed around attraction, while ARCHITECTURE rewards discovery and lingering. Together they demonstrate how a highly visible commercial environment can create a context for a much quieter and lowkey cultural space.

Gourmega makes this relationship more explicit. Located in the West Village, the 14-seat supper club offers a $300 tasting menu within a dark, intimate interior designed by Mariam Issoufou Kamara and Jon Gray. Gourmega operates through Rethink Food’s Sustainable Community Kitchen, using the revenue generated by the restaurant to support a much larger food-distribution system. The kitchen produces tens of thousands of meals each week for churches, shelters, and community organizations across New York City. The highly exclusive dining experience occupies only a small portion of the operation while its economic activity supports a much larger communal food infrastructure.

The history of the West Village adds another layer to this relationship. The area was historically associated with Black landowners and communities and later became an important site for Black and brown nightlife and cultural life. Gourmega now operates within a neighborhood associated with a much wealthier and predominantly white demographic. Its model places these different histories and economic conditions within the same system, using luxury consumption to generate resources for food access.

These case studies suggest a model in which commercial success is a starting point for other forms of production. Community Goods uses popularity and cultural visibility to fund a quieter archival space. Gourmega uses exclusivity and luxury to support community food infrastructure. Both separate the economic intensity of one activity from the cultural or social value of another.

Their economic power also comes with a contradiction. Virality, exclusivity, and celebrity create desire and revenue, while simultaneously producing spaces that can feel inaccessible to the communities they serve. Gourmega and ARCHITECTURE show how commercial engines can produce cultural products. The visibility and funding generated by one activity can support spaces that prioritize culture, experimentation, or community over profit.
